# Architecture plan

## Repository layout

Use a Cargo workspace plus an independently managed frontend:

```text
.
├── Cargo.toml                    # workspace: backend, worker, shared
├── crates/
│   ├── backend/                  # Axum HTTP server and static-file hosting
│   │   └── src/{api,config,db,services}/
│   ├── worker/                   # scheduled ingestion executable
│   │   └── src/{config,jobs}/
│   └── shared/                   # domain types, config primitives, contracts
│       └── src/{domain,normalization}/
├── frontend/                     # React, TypeScript, Tailwind, React Query
│   └── src/{api,components,pages,theme}/
├── migrations/                   # ordered sqlx PostgreSQL migrations
├── scripts/                      # local run/build/deployment helpers
├── tests/{fixtures,e2e}/          # ingestion fixtures and end-to-end coverage
└── docs/                         # operator documentation
```

Keep database and infrastructure adapters in the backend/worker crates, while
`shared` contains no HTTP, SQL, storage, or vendor-client dependency. The
frontend consumes explicit JSON DTOs owned by the backend API rather than Rust
internal structs directly.

## Core data and contracts

PostgreSQL is the system of record:

- `sources`: tracked source configuration (`region`, `agency`, base URL, crawl
  configuration, enabled state).
- `policy_entries`: the current normalized identity/state of a policy item,
  uniquely scoped to source; indexed for latest-change ordering and
  region/agency/status filters.
- `policy_versions`: immutable observed versions with change kind (`new` or
  `updated`), normalized-content hash, observed timestamp, summary, and raw
  snapshot object key/reference.

The canonical normalization boundary is a `NormalizedPolicyRecord` with title,
region, agency, effective/publication date, status, source URL, stable external
identifier, and a deterministic canonical-content representation/hash.
Source-specific normalizers implement one common interface. Change detection
compares the canonical content/hash with the latest version and writes a new
version only for new or updated records.

Raw fetched bytes are stored in object storage first, under deterministic,
partitioned keys (source/date/content hash); the version row stores the key and
content metadata. A failed snapshot, fetch, normalization, or summary is
recorded per source/run without halting other sources.

Public API contracts:

- `GET /health`: deploy readiness/status.
- `GET /api/entries`: cursor-paginated, reverse-chronological change feed;
  optional `region`, `agency`, and `status` filters.
- `GET /api/entries/:id`: entry metadata, versions, and summaries, shaped for
  the expandable panel and a later full-diff view.

The worker orchestrates `fetch -> snapshot -> normalize -> detect/version ->
summarize` for each enabled source. The summarizer is a fallible, timeout-bound
interface; an unavailable AI service never prevents the version write and can
yield a null/fallback summary. Scheduler cadence is configuration-driven.

## Delivery dependencies

1. Foundation: #1 workspace layout, then #2 tooling, #3 sqlx/migrations, and
   #4 environment configuration; #5 uses all of these to provide a running
   server and static frontend deployment.
2. Data and ingestion: #6 sources -> #7 entries -> #8 versions; #9 attaches
   snapshot storage to versions; #10 scheduler -> #11 fetch/snapshot -> #12
   and #13 normalizers -> #14 change detection. #15 summarizer -> #16 stored
   change summaries. #26 hardens this whole pipeline.
3. API: #17 feed endpoint depends on persisted versions/summaries; #18 adds
   filters; #19 exposes entry detail/history.
4. UI: #20 scaffold/client/theme -> #21 feed -> #22 filters and #23 detail ->
   #24 local last-seen highlighting.
5. Assurance and operations: #25 exercises the end-to-end path after the
   ingestion, API, and UI layers; #27 documents and prepares the bare deploy
   after the foundation is usable.

## Key decisions

- Use `sqlx` migrations and compile-time-safe query patterns where practical;
  all database access is behind repository/service boundaries.
- Use a stable source external ID plus source ID for idempotency, and a
  canonical-content hash for change classification; avoid treating fetch time
  as a substantive change.
- Separate domain records, persistence models, and API DTOs so migrations and
  UI needs can evolve independently.
- Serve the built frontend through Axum in the bare deployment. Preserve proxy
  semantics: derive public host/protocol from forwarded headers, and do not
  reject own-origin browser static assets based on the rewritten internal Host.
- Keep anonymous last-seen state in browser local storage; it is a presentation
  feature and does not alter feed ordering or server-side records.
- Make E2E tests deterministic with fixture HTTP responses, isolated database
  state, fake object storage/summarizer adapters, and assertions from ingestion
  through rendered feed output.
